---
import Docs from "../../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import Experimental from "../../../components/Experimental.astro";

const writingRules = [
  {
    name: "One sitting", gateLabel: "judgment-only",
    plain: "A spec must be small enough that the PO can read and judge the contract in a single review sitting. Human attention is the appetite unit — appetite governs the spec's reviewability, not the build's size. A large atomic build with a tight spec is eligible. If you can't review it in one pass, split it or decompose it (parent-map); slugs are cheap.",
  },
  {
    name: "Falsifiable or draft", gateLabel: "specline · advisory",
    plain: "Every acceptance check must be settleable the same way twice; \"Improve the dashboard\" names nothing anyone can settle. An item only a person can settle is marked under a ### human sub-heading. Specline surfaces an unfalsifiable or absent acceptance as advisory — it's the decider's call, not a block.",
  },
  {
    name: "Intent over description", gateLabel: "advisory · spec-critic",
    plain: "Documentation records what code cannot say: goals, non-goals, rejected options, evidence, rules, boundaries. Once code exists it is the source of truth for mechanics, and prescribed mechanics then compete with it and go stale — the same reason the decider's go belongs to git, not a field: don't model what another source of truth owns. This is a tradeoff, not a law, and it isn't measured. A pre-build spec with no code yet may legitimately carry worked mechanics. So Specline flags it as advice; the decider decides.",
  },
];

const experimental = [
  {
    name: "The Coupling Ceiling", gateLabel: "specline · warn",
    plain: "A spec plus everything its relations force an agent to load must fit under the repo's pinned ceiling with room left to think — measured once, at shaping time, as a structural property, not a runtime budget. With million-token windows the binding constraint isn't fitting but signal quality: irrelevant forced loads degrade a frontier model's output. Read a breach as \"too entangled\" — slice it or decouple it.",
  },
  {
    name: "Staleness", gateLabel: "specline · advisory",
    plain: "building and blocked each carry a stale_after date — the point past which an untouched build is presumed abandoned, not the point it is discarded. Going stale flags the spec: Specline warns so a human notices an abandoned build — it never blocks. Exit is an explicit reshape (a fresh approving merge) or kill.",
  },
  {
    name: "The Decider Budget", gateLabel: "specline · advisory",
    plain: "A named decider may have at most 3 specs in building and 6 in active states (building | blocked) at once, per-repo override allowed. Agent capacity is not the constraint; the decider's queue is. WIP limits on humans, not on machines — Specline warns over the ceiling, never blocks.",
  },
];
---
<Docs title="Reference — the boundaries">
  <div class="doc-kicker">Reference · Boundaries</div>
  <h1>The boundaries</h1>
  <p class="doc-lead">
    Scrum rations human time; Shape Up rations risk. Specline rations <strong>judgment</strong>.
    Three rules govern <em>writing</em> a spec, and one governs <em>keeping the record</em> —
    everything else is implementation. (A named decider budget, a coupling ceiling, and
    staleness timers exist too, but they govern <em>unattended and parallel</em> builds — an
    experimental, opt-in part of the canon; see below.)
  </p>

  <h2>Writing rules</h2>
  {writingRules.map((b) => (
    <div class="bcard">
      <h3>{b.name} <span class="gate-tag gate-human">{b.gateLabel}</span></h3>
      <p>{b.plain}</p>
    </div>
  ))}

  <h2>The record rule</h2>
  <div class="bcard">
    <h3>Never delete a decision <span class="gate-tag gate-doctor">specline · integrity</span></h3>
    <p>An approved slug is permanent and archive is immutable: when something ships, a knowledge doc lands and the spec is archived; when it is killed, it is archived with a one-line tombstone. Nothing is bare-deleted. This is the one rule the integrity checks exist to protect, and the reason every approved slug resolves forever.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout"><span class="ci">▸</span><p><strong>Two enforcers, one rule set.</strong> Specline is deterministic — it checks structure (Falsifiable or draft, the record rule). Meaning — one-sitting sizing, mechanics-creep — is judgment, and lives at the two human gates (approve, accept). Specline adds assurance, not validity.</p></div>

  <h2>Experimental — unattended &amp; parallel builds</h2>
  <Experimental />
  {experimental.map((b) => (
    <div class="bcard">
      <h3>{b.name} <span class="gate-tag gate-doctor">{b.gateLabel}</span></h3>
      <p>{b.plain}</p>
    </div>
  ))}

  <div class="callout"><span class="ci">▸</span><p>Up: <a href="/handbook/four-actors">handbook — four actors, one line</a>. The law: <a href="/spec.md">the canon, "Writing rules" and "The record rule"</a>.</p></div>
</Docs>
